this is bangkok's first governor  election since the coup that brought the  military to power in 2013.  the military removed the previous  elected governor in 2016 to install  police general assoin quan wong in the  job now he says he's running as an  independent candidate though many still  see him as the chosen nominee of the  military  i want to continue serving the people  it's common to run for an office for  another term i know that i was appointed  but now i want to be elected  guan wong's perceived closeness to the  coup leaders may cost him at the polls  his main rival engineer and former  minister chad  point  enjoys wide support among young people  who filled the streets on many occasions  to protest against the military coup and  demand reform he says he has a vision  for a more modernized more prosperous  capital  it doesn't matter if you're an  independent candidate or backed by  political parties the governor has to  remove his allegiance to a political  party and has to try his or her best to  take care of the people  city points young supporters who failed  to have their demands met through  protests see this election as a likely  opportunity for change they don't want  to miss it  this is a mall where there is a huge  crackdown on young protesters it's  probably the first clash between  protesters and police officers do you  think that those kids who came out to  fight on the street would not come out  to vote  i think it will show that young people  care about politics and they are aware  of what's going on they are not just  someone who hides behind the keyboard it  is time for us to choose who should lead  us and how the city will move forward  the last bangkok governor election was  in 2013  and for nine years opposition voters  have been waiting for a day like this  hoping it will allow them to get by the  ballot box what was denied them by force  since the military coup muhammad
 

